  {
    course: "BI110",
    term: "T2",
    front: "What is the great plate count anomaly?",
    back: "## The Invisible Majority\n\n**The great plate count anomaly is the gap between the number of bacteria visible under a microscope and the number that can be grown on agar plates.**\n\nCount a water or soil sample under a microscope and you may see millions of bacterial cells. Plate the same sample on nutrient agar and you may grow only a few hundred colonies. The vast majority of bacteria in nature do not grow under standard lab conditions.\n\nThe reason is nutritional. Most bacteria in nature live in complex communities, depending on nutrients and signals from neighbours. When isolated on a nutrient-rich agar plate, they cannot adjust to the artificial conditions or they are outcompeted by fast-growing species.\n\nThis is why our catalog of bacterial species (around 5,200) is so much smaller than the estimated total (millions). Metagenomics -- sequencing DNA directly from environmental samples -- bypasses the culture bottleneck. But a DNA sequence is not a specimen, and a species cannot be formally named without one."
  }
