    back: "## Bacillus anthracis\n\n**Anthrax is caused by Bacillus anthracis, a Gram-positive, rod-shaped, spore-forming bacterium.**\n\nThe bacterium lives in soil as a spore. When grazing animals ingest the spores, they develop anthrax and die. The spores return to the soil when the carcass decays, and the cycle continues. Spores can survive for decades in contaminated soil, making certain pastures permanently dangerous for livestock.\n\nIn humans, anthrax usually enters through the skin (cutaneous anthrax), through the lungs (inhalation anthrax), or through the gut (gastrointestinal anthrax). Cutaneous anthrax is the most common and least dangerous; inhalation anthrax is the most dangerous, with a high mortality rate even with treatment.\n\nBacillus anthracis is a potential bioweapon because its spores are highly stable and can be aerosolised. The 2001 anthrax attacks in the United States demonstrated this risk.\n\n> Not just 'Bacillus' -- that is the genus, and other Bacillus species are harmless."
