    back: "## Vibrio cholerae\n\n**Cholera is caused by Vibrio cholerae, a Gram-negative, comma-shaped bacterium.**\n\nThe bacterium produces a toxin called cholera toxin, which targets the cells of the small intestine. The toxin forces the cells to secrete chloride ions, and water follows by osmosis. The result is massive watery diarrhoea -- the classic 'rice-water stool' -- and rapid dehydration.\n\nCholera spreads through contaminated water and food. It is a disease of poor sanitation; the bacterium is present in the stool of infected people, and if that stool reaches drinking water, an outbreak follows. The 19th-century cholera pandemics in Europe and the ongoing outbreaks in Africa and Asia have all been waterborne.\n\nTreatment is rehydration -- replacing water and electrolytes faster than they are lost. Oral rehydration solution is simple, cheap, and highly effective. Antibiotics shorten the illness, but they are secondary to rehydration.\n\nPrevention is water treatment and sanitation. Cholera has been eliminated from most developed countries by chlorinated water supplies."
