    term: "T2",
    front: "What causes syphilis?",
    back: "## Treponema pallidum\n\n**Syphilis is caused by Treponema pallidum, a spirochete with a corkscrew shape.**\n\nThe bacterium is extremely thin (about 0.1 micrometres wide) and long (6-15 micrometres), with tight spirals. It is Gram-negative but difficult to stain because its thinness and outer sheath interfere with dye uptake. Darkfield microscopy is the standard way to visualise it.\n\nSyphilis spreads through direct contact -- usually sexual contact, but also from mother to fetus (congenital syphilis). The bacterium penetrates mucous membranes and skin, then enters the bloodstream and spreads throughout the body.\n\nThe disease has stages. Primary syphilis produces a chancre at the site of infection. Secondary syphilis produces a body-wide rash. Latent syphilis is silent. Tertiary syphilis -- sometimes decades later -- can damage the heart, brain, and other organs.\n\nPenicillin is the treatment, and it is highly effective at all stages. The challenge is diagnosis: the disease can be silent for years while doing damage."
