    course: "BI110",
    term: "T2",
    front: "What causes tuberculosis?",
    back: "## Mycobacterium tuberculosis\n\n**Tuberculosis is caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis, a slow-growing, aerobic bacterium with a waxy cell wall.**\n\nThe cell wall is rich in mycolic acids, which makes the bacterium resistant to many antibiotics and difficult to stain. It does not reliably Gram-stain; it does not reliably acid-fast stain with standard techniques. Special staining -- the Ziehl-Neelsen stain or auramine-rhodamine fluorescence -- is required.\n\nThe bacterium primarily infects the lungs, causing a chronic cough, fever, night sweats, and weight loss. It spreads through airborne droplets from infected people. The bacteria are engulfed by lung macrophages but survive inside them, hiding from the immune system. Over time, granulomas form, walling off the bacteria.\n\nLatent TB -- infection without active disease -- can last for years. About a quarter of the world's population has latent TB, and about 10% will develop active disease in their lifetime, mostly when immunity weakens.\n\nTreatment requires a multi-drug regimen for six months or more. Because the bacterium is slow-growing and mutates rapidly, single-drug therapy produces resistance. Multidrug-resistant TB (MDR-TB) is a major global health problem."
