    term: "T2",
    front: "What causes bacterial pneumonia?",
    back: "## Streptococcus pneumoniae\n\n**The most common cause of bacterial pneumonia is Streptococcus pneumoniae, a Gram-positive diplococcus.**\n\nThe bacterium is part of the normal flora of the upper respiratory tract -- many people carry it harmlessly. It causes disease when it reaches the lungs, usually after a viral infection weakens the body's defences.\n\nOnce in the lungs, the bacteria multiply in the alveoli and trigger inflammation. Fluid and immune cells accumulate, blocking oxygen exchange. The result is fever, cough, chest pain, and difficulty breathing. In elderly or immunocompromised patients, it can be fatal.\n\nThe polysaccharide capsule is the main virulence factor. Strains with a capsule resist phagocytosis and cause severe disease. Strains without a capsule are usually harmless. This is why the pneumococcal vaccine is based on capsule polysaccharides -- it teaches the immune system to recognise and attack the capsule.\n\nPenicillin works against most strains, but resistance is increasing. Multi-drug resistant pneumococcus is now a concern in many countries."
