    course: "BI110",
    term: "T2",
    front: "What diseases does E. coli cause?",
    back: "## Many Illnesses\n\n**Escherichia coli causes urinary tract infections, gastroenteritis, neonatal meningitis, and sepsis.**\n\nMost E. coli strains are harmless or beneficial -- they are part of the normal gut flora and help digest food. But some strains are pathogenic, and they can cause disease in various ways.\n\nUrinary tract infections (UTIs) are the most common E. coli disease. The bacterium migrates from the gut to the urinary tract, attaches to the bladder wall using pili, and triggers inflammation. Women are more susceptible than men because of the shorter urethra.\n\nCertain strains -- particularly E. coli O157:H7 -- produce toxins that damage the gut lining. This causes severe diarrhoea (sometimes bloody), abdominal cramps, and in some cases haemolytic uraemic syndrome (HUS), a life-threatening kidney complication. O157:H7 is associated with undercooked beef and contaminated vegetables.\n\nNeonatal meningitis is a rarer but very serious disease. E. coli from the mother's gut can infect the newborn during delivery, causing meningitis that can lead to lifelong disability."
