    course: "BI110",
    term: "T2",
    front: "How is cholera transmitted?",
    back: "## Contaminated Water and Food\n\n**Cholera is transmitted through contaminated water and food, especially where sanitation is poor.**\n\nThe bacterium is shed in the stool of infected people. If that stool reaches drinking water -- through lack of sewage treatment, flooding, or poor hygiene -- the bacterium spreads to everyone who drinks the water. This is why cholera outbreaks follow natural disasters, wars, and infrastructure breakdowns.\n\nThe classic case is a contaminated municipal water supply. In the 19th century, cholera devastated London until John Snow traced an outbreak to a contaminated water pump -- one of the founding moments of epidemiology. Modern outbreaks in Haiti, Yemen, and Sub-Saharan Africa follow the same pattern: drinking water contaminated by human waste.\n\nFood can also be a route. Shellfish harvested from contaminated water, vegetables irrigated with contaminated water, and street food prepared without proper hygiene can all carry the bacterium. Person-to-person transmission is possible but less common -- the infectious dose is high, so it takes significant contamination to cause disease.\n\nThe bacterium is highly susceptible to stomach acid, so a person needs to ingest millions of cells to be infected. This is why a healthy stomach provides some protection -- but not enough when the contamination is severe."
