    term: "T2",
    front: "What is the mucilaginous sheath?",
    back: "## The Slime Layer\n\n**The mucilaginous sheath is a gelatinous layer surrounding cyanobacterial cells and filaments.**\n\nIt is made of complex polysaccharides, sometimes pigmented yellow or brown. The sheath holds cells together in filamentous and colonial forms, protects them from drying out, and helps them glide over surfaces.\n\nIn filamentous cyanobacteria like Oscillatoria and Nostoc, the sheath wraps the entire filament, giving it a smooth outer surface. The filament can move within the sheath, allowing it to glide toward light.\n\nThe sheath is also protective against environmental stress. It retains water during dry spells, buffers against pH or temperature change, and (in some species) absorbs harmful UV radiation.\n\nCyanobacteria that form mats in ponds, hot springs, or shallow marine environments rely on the sheath to stabilise the colony. The visible green slick on a pond surface is often a mat of sheathed filaments."
