    front: "What are the two growth forms of cyanobacteria?",
    back: "## Single Cells and Filaments\n\n**Cyanobacteria grow either as single cells (some colonial) or as filamentous chains of cells.**\n\nUnicellular forms live as individual cells, sometimes grouped in colonies held together by the sheath. Synechococcus and Prochlorococcus are common unicellular cyanobacteria in the ocean, responsible for a significant fraction of global primary production.\n\nFilamentous forms grow as chains of cells end-to-end, surrounded by a continuous sheath. Oscillatoria forms long, unbranched filaments that glide with a slow oscillation. Nostoc forms filaments that curl and fold, sometimes binding into macroscopic jelly-like masses. Anabaena forms chains of round cells with occasional larger cells called heterocysts.\n\nThe filamentous form is particularly important because it allows cell specialisation. Some cells in the filament can become heterocysts for nitrogen fixation, while the rest photosynthesise. This is a simple form of division of labour."
