    back: "## The Third Domain\n\n**Archaea are single-celled prokaryotic organisms forming one of the three domains of life, distinct from Bacteria and Eukaryota.**\n\nThey look like bacteria under a microscope, small, simple, no nucleus, but their molecular biology is fundamentally different. Their cell walls lack peptidoglycan. Their membrane lipids use ether bonds rather than ester bonds. Their rRNA sequences diverge deeply from both bacteria and eukaryotes.\n\nThese differences were invisible until molecular tools arrived in the 1970s. When Carl Woese compared ribosomal RNA from different prokaryotes, he found that some were so different from bacteria that they deserved their own domain. The three-domain system was born.\n\nArchaea are found in extreme environments, hot springs, salt lakes, hydrothermal vents, but also in ordinary soils, oceans, and even the human gut. They are not all extremophiles.\n\n> It is easy to say Monera, that is an obsolete kingdom. Archaea is a domain, separate from Bacteria, and it was only recognised as such in 1990."
