    term: "T2",
    front: "How do archaeal membrane lipids differ from bacterial ones?",
    back: "## Ether Bonds and Branched Chains\n\n**Archaeal membrane lipids use ether bonds and branched isoprenoid chains; bacterial lipids use ester bonds and straight fatty acid chains.**\n\nThe difference matters physically. Ether bonds are more resistant to chemical and thermal breakdown than ester bonds. Branched chains pack more tightly in the membrane, making it more stable at high temperatures.\n\nThis is why archaeal membranes can function at temperatures that would destroy bacterial membranes. Hyperthermophilic archaea live at 80-100°C; their ether-branched lipids keep the membrane intact when ordinary phospholipids would melt or fall apart.\n\nThe chemical difference is also taxonomically diagnostic. If you analyse the membrane lipids of an unknown prokaryote and find ether-linked branched chains, it is an archaeon. If you find ester-linked straight chains, it is a bacterium.\n\nA few archaea have membrane-spanning tetraether lipids that form a monolayer rather than a bilayer. This is even more stable and is found in the most heat-tolerant species.\n\n> People often say 'thicker', the membrane is not necessarily thicker; it is chemically different."
