    back: "## Life at the Limits\n\n**An extremophile is an organism that thrives in environmental conditions that would kill most other life, extreme heat, cold, salt, acidity, alkalinity, pressure, or radiation.**\n\nExtremophiles are found across all three domains, but archaea are the most famous. Some archaea live at 100°C, others at pH 0, others in saturated salt. Bacteria have extremophiles too (Deinococcus radiodurans survives high radiation), and so do some eukaryotes (tardigrades survive desiccation and vacuum).\n\nThe word 'extremophile' does not mean 'extremely different.' It means 'lover of extremes', the organism requires the extreme condition, not just tolerates it. A hyperthermophile from a hot spring cannot grow at 37°C; the heat is required.\n\nThe study of extremophiles has practical importance. Their enzymes (extremozymes) work under conditions that would denature ordinary enzymes, so they are valuable in industrial processes, PCR, and biotechnology."
