    term: "T2",
    front: "What is a retrovirus?",
    back: "## RNA to DNA\n\n**A retrovirus is an RNA virus that converts its RNA genome into DNA inside the host cell, using the enzyme reverse transcriptase.**\n\nThe normal flow of genetic information is DNA → RNA → protein. Retroviruses reverse the first step. Their RNA genome is copied into DNA, which then integrates into the host chromosome.\n\nThis integration makes the infection permanent. Once the viral DNA is in the host genome, it cannot be removed. The host cell carries it for life.\n\nHIV is the most famous retrovirus, but it is not the only one. Human T-lymphotropic virus (HTLV-1) causes adult T-cell leukemia. Endogenous retroviruses, viral sequences integrated into the human genome millions of years ago, make up about 8% of human DNA.\n\nThe retroviral genome has three essential genes: gag (structural proteins), pol (enzymes, including reverse transcriptase and integrase), and env (envelope glycoproteins). These three genes are enough to hijack a host cell and produce new virions.\n\n> People often say 'retroviruses cause AIDS', only HIV causes AIDS; not all retroviruses do."
