    course: "BI110",
    term: "T2",
    front: "What does integrase do in HIV?",
    back: "## The Integration Enzyme\n\n**Integrase is the enzyme that inserts the viral DNA into the host cell's chromosome, making the infection permanent.**\n\nAfter reverse transcriptase converts the viral RNA into double-stranded DNA, the DNA must be integrated into the host genome. Integrase catalyses this step. It cuts the host DNA at a specific site and inserts the viral DNA, sealing the ends together.\n\nThe integrated viral DNA is called a provirus. It is now part of the host genome, indistinguishable in principle from the host's own genes. Every time the cell divides, the provirus is copied along with the host DNA, and every daughter cell inherits it.\n\nThis integration is what makes HIV incurable. Even if every virus particle is cleared from the blood, the integrated provirus remains in the host cells. As long as those cells live, they can produce new viruses when activated.\n\nIntegrase is the target of a class of HIV drugs called integrase inhibitors, raltegravir, dolutegravir, and others. These are among the most effective drugs in modern HIV treatment, blocking integration and preventing the establishment of new proviruses."
