    back: "## The Catch-All Eukaryotes\n\n**Kingdom Protista is a diverse group of eukaryotic organisms that do not fit into the kingdoms Plantae, Animalia, or Fungi.**\n\nThe name was introduced by Ernst Haeckel in 1886 to hold organisms that were neither plant nor animal. Modern protists include single-celled eukaryotes, some colonial forms, and some multicellular algae.\n\nThe group is not a natural evolutionary unit. Molecular data shows that some protists are more closely related to plants than to other protists; others are closer to animals; others to fungi. Protista is called 'polyphyletic', a collection of lineages that do not share a recent common ancestor.\n\nDespite this, the kingdom remains in textbooks because it is useful for organisation. It groups the 'simple' eukaryotes, the ones that do not fit into the more familiar kingdoms."
