    front: "What are algae in the protist sense?",
    back: "## Plant-Like Protists\n\n**Algae are autotrophic, photosynthetic protists that resemble plants in their nutrition.**\n\nThe name comes from Latin 'alga,' meaning seaweed. Algae are found in aquatic environments, freshwater, marine, and moist soil. They range from single-celled forms (like Chlamydomonas) to multicellular forms (like kelp and sea lettuce).\n\nAlgae are the primary producers of aquatic ecosystems. They use sunlight to make food and release oxygen, roughly half the oxygen in the atmosphere comes from algal photosynthesis. They are also the base of the aquatic food web, providing energy for zooplankton, small fish, and other organisms.\n\nAlgae are classified into several phyla based on pigmentation, storage products, and cell wall composition. The main phyla are Chlorophyta (green algae), Rhodophyta (red algae), Phaeophyta (brown algae), Chrysophyta (diatoms), and Pyrrophyta (dinoflagellates).\n\nAlgae are not a single evolutionary group. The various phyla evolved independently from different heterotrophic protist ancestors. They are grouped by their shared feature (photosynthesis), not by ancestry."
