  {
    course: "BI110",
    term: "T2",
    front: "What is Phylum Rhodophyta?",
    back: "## Red Algae\n\n**Rhodophyta is the phylum of red algae, marine algae that contain phycoerythrin, a red pigment, in addition to chlorophyll.**\n\nRed algae are almost all multicellular and almost all marine. They are found in coastal waters, often at depths where other algae cannot grow because the light is too dim. Their red pigment (phycoerythrin) absorbs blue-green light, which penetrates deeper into water than red or orange light, so red algae can photosynthesise at depths where other algae cannot.\n\nThe cell walls are made of cellulose, often with additional sulfated polysaccharides (agar, carrageenan). These substances are valuable commercially, agar is used in microbiology, and carrageenan is used in food and cosmetics.\n\nRed algae include some of the most economically important species. Nori (Porphyra) is a food crop in Japan, Korea, and China. Gelidium is the primary source of agar. Chondrus crispus (Irish moss) is used for carrageenan.\n\nRed algae are not always red, they can be green, blue, or purple, depending on the ratio of pigments and the light environment. The phylum name reflects the most common colour, but the actual colour varies.\n\n> It is easy to say 'brown algae', brown algae (Phaeophyta) produce algin, not agar."
  },
  {
    course: "BI110",
    term: "T2",
    front: "Where does agar come from?",
    back: "## From Red Algae\n\n**Agar is extracted from red algae, particularly from the genus Gelidium and related species.**\n\nThe algae are harvested from coastal waters (mostly in Japan, Korea, Spain, and Portugal), dried, and processed to extract the agar. The process involves boiling the algae in water, filtering out the solids, and cooling the extract. The agar solidifies into a gel, which is then dried and powdered.\n\nAgar is a polysaccharide, a mixture of agarose and agaropectin. It has unique properties that make it valuable. It melts at about 85°C and solidifies at about 35-40°C, so it stays solid at room temperature but can be easily melted and poured. And it is not degraded by most microorganisms, so it provides a stable medium for bacterial growth.\n\nThe main use is in microbiology. Agar plates, petri dishes filled with agar medium, are the standard tool for growing bacteria, fungi, and other microbes. The agar provides a solid surface, the nutrients in the medium support growth, and the microbes form visible colonies.\n\nOther uses include food (as a thickener in desserts and jellies), cosmetics (as a gelling agent), and pharmaceuticals (as a base for capsules and suppositories)."
  },
  {
    course: "BI110",
    term: "T2",
    front: "What is Phylum Phaeophyta?",
    back: "## Brown Algae\n\n**Phaeophyta is the phylum of brown algae, mostly multicellular, mostly marine, with brown pigments (fucoxanthin) in addition to chlorophyll.**\n\nBrown algae are common in coastal waters, especially in cold or temperate regions. The largest species, the giant kelp (Macrocystis pyrifera), can grow 45 metres long and form underwater forests. Smaller species, like Sargassum, grow on rocks and form mats in the intertidal zone.\n\nThe brown colour comes from fucoxanthin, an accessory pigment that absorbs blue-green light and gives the algae their characteristic brown or olive tint. This pigment is especially useful in coastal waters, where the light spectrum is shifted toward blue-green.\n\nThe cell walls are made of cellulose and algin, a polysaccharide that gives brown algae their flexibility and toughness. Algin is extracted commercially for use in food (ice cream, sauces), cosmetics, and industrial applications.\n\nBrown algae are important ecologically. Kelp forests provide habitat for fish, invertebrates, and marine mammals. They also produce large amounts of organic matter, which enters the food web when the kelp dies and decays."
  },
  {
    course: "BI110",
    term: "T2",
    front: "What is algin used for?",
    back: "## A Versatile Thickener\n\n**Algin is a polysaccharide extracted from brown algae, used as a thickener, stabiliser, and gelling agent in food, cosmetics, and industry.**\n\nAlgin is extracted from brown algae by treating the dried algae with alkali, then precipitating the alginate with acid or calcium salts. The extracted algin is a white powder that dissolves in water to form a viscous solution.\n\nIn food, algin is used as a thickener and stabiliser. It improves the texture of ice cream, preventing ice crystals from forming. It thickens sauces and dressings. It stabilises foam in beer. It is also used in 'molecular gastronomy' to make edible spheres (spherification).\n\nIn cosmetics, algin is used in lotions and creams, where it provides a smooth, non-greasy feel. In medicine, it is used in wound dressings (alginate dressings), where it absorbs fluid and forms a gel that keeps the wound moist.\n\nIn industry, algin is used in textile printing, paper manufacturing, and welding rods. Its ability to thicken water and form gels makes it versatile across many sectors.\n\nAlgin is one of the most widely used algal products, alongside agar and carrageenan. All three are extracted from different algal phyla."
  },
  {
    course: "BI110",
    term: "T2",
    front: "What is Phylum Chrysophyta?",
    back: "## Diatoms\n\n**Chrysophyta is the phylum of diatoms, single-celled algae with silica shells called frustules.**\n\nDiatoms are among the most important primary producers in aquatic ecosystems. They are found in both freshwater and marine environments, and they account for a significant fraction of global primary production, some estimates suggest 20-30% of the oxygen in the atmosphere comes from diatoms.\n\nThe silica shell (frustule) is the defining feature. It is made of silica, has intricate patterns, and is preserved long after the cell dies. When diatoms die, their shells sink to the bottom of lakes and oceans, forming thick deposits called diatomaceous earth.\n\nDiatoms store food as oils, which are lighter than starch or glycogen and help the cell float at the surface where light is available. This buoyancy regulation is essential for a photosynthetic organism that cannot swim.\n\nThe frustules have many industrial uses. Diatomaceous earth is a natural filter, used in water purification and in pool filters. It is also an abrasive, used in toothpaste and metal polishing, and a filler, used in paints and plastics.\n\nDiatoms are important in paleoclimatology. The shell composition and species distribution in sediment cores can reconstruct past ocean temperatures, salinities, and nutrient levels."
  },
  {
    course: "BI110",
    term: "T2",
    front: "What is diatomaceous earth?",
    back: "## Fossilised Diatoms\n\n**Diatomaceous earth is a powdery deposit of fossilised diatom shells, used as a filter, abrasive, and filler.**\n\nThe deposit forms over millions of years. Diatoms live in lakes and oceans, and when they die, their silica shells sink to the bottom. Over time, the shells accumulate into thick layers, sometimes hundreds of metres thick, which are mined for commercial use.\n\nThe material is essentially pure silica (SiO₂), with the intricate shapes of the original frustules preserved. It is lightweight, porous, and chemically inert, properties that make it useful in many applications.\n\nAs a filter, diatomaceous earth traps particles as small as 1 micrometre. It is used in water purification, beer and wine filtration, and swimming pool filters. As an abrasive, its fine, sharp particles polish surfaces without scratching deeply. It is used in toothpaste, metal polish, and insecticidal dusts (the sharp particles damage insect cuticles, causing dehydration).\n\nDiatomaceous earth is also used in industry. It is a filler in paint, plastic, and rubber. It is a thermal insulator, used in high-temperature equipment. It is a mild absorbent, used to clean up spills.\n\nThe material is considered safe for human use (when not inhaled). It is approved as a food additive in many countries, where it is used as an anti-caking agent."
  },
  {
    course: "BI110",
    term: "T2",
    front: "What is Phylum Pyrrophyta?",
    back: "## Dinoflagellates\n\n**Pyrrophyta is the phylum of dinoflagellates, single-celled algae with two flagella, known for bioluminescence and red tides.**\n\nThe name dinoflagellate means 'whirling whip', a reference to the two flagella, one of which beats in a groove around the cell, causing it to rotate as it swims. The whirling motion is distinctive and gives the group its name.\n\nMost dinoflagellates are marine. They are common in coastal waters, where they contribute to primary production. Some species are photosynthetic; others are heterotrophic; some are mixotrophic.\n\nDinoflagellates are best known for red tides. When they grow rapidly (usually due to nutrient pollution), they can turn the water red or brown. Some species produce neurotoxins, saxitoxin, for example, which accumulate in shellfish and can cause paralytic shellfish poisoning in humans.\n\nBioluminescence is another distinctive feature. Many dinoflagellates can emit light (a blue-green glow) when disturbed. The phenomenon is visible as glowing waves at night and has been studied for its ecological role and its potential biotechnological applications.\n\nDinoflagellates are also involved in symbiosis. Coral reefs depend on zooxanthellae, a group of photosynthetic dinoflagellates that live inside coral cells and supply the coral with nutrients."
  },
  {
    course: "BI110",
    term: "T2",
    front: "What causes red tides?",
    back: "## Dinoflagellate Blooms\n\n**Red tides are caused by blooms of dinoflagellates, which multiply rapidly and discolour the water red or brown.**\n\nThe bloom is triggered by nutrient pollution, usually from agricultural runoff, sewage, or other human sources. When the water contains excess nutrients (nitrogen and phosphorus), the dinoflagellates grow rapidly, reaching densities of millions of cells per litre.\n\nThe colour comes from the pigments in the dinoflagellate cells. Some species are red, others brown, yellow, or green. When they reach high density, the colour becomes visible at the surface.\n\nMany red tide species produce toxins. Saxitoxin (from Alexandrium) and brevetoxin (from Karenia brevis) are neurotoxins that can kill fish, birds, and marine mammals. In humans, the toxins cause paralytic shellfish poisoning (from eating contaminated shellfish) or respiratory irritation (from breathing aerosolized toxins).\n\nRed tides are not always harmful. Some blooms are benign, but some are toxic. The distinction depends on the species involved.\n\nRed tides have increased in frequency and severity in recent decades, likely due to increased nutrient pollution and warming waters. They are now a global concern for fisheries, aquaculture, and human health."
  },
  {
    course: "BI110",
    term: "T2",
    front: "What is bioluminescence?",
    back: "## Living Light\n\n**Bioluminescence is the production of light by a living organism through a chemical reaction.**\n\nThe reaction involves a light-emitting molecule (luciferin) and an enzyme (luciferase). When luciferin is oxidised by luciferase in the presence of oxygen, the energy released is emitted as light. The colour of the light depends on the luciferin and luciferase involved, usually blue-green in marine organisms, but sometimes yellow, red, or other colours.\n\nBioluminescence is common in marine environments. Dinoflagellates are the most well-known bioluminescent protists, but many other organisms use it too, jellyfish, fireflies, some squid, and deep-sea fish.\n\nThe function of bioluminescence varies. In dinoflagellates, it may be a defence mechanism, the flash of light can startle predators or attract larger predators that eat the dinoflagellate's predators. In other organisms, it may be used for mating, camouflage, or luring prey.\n\nBioluminescence has practical applications. It is used in research as a reporter system, scientists can attach luciferase genes to other genes to track their expression. It is also being studied for use in biosensors and imaging.\n\nThe phenomenon is one of the most striking examples of the diversity of protists. It shows that algae are not just passive photosynthetic cells, they interact with their environment in complex ways."
  },
  {
    course: "BI110",
    term: "T2",
    front: "How do algae reproduce?",
    back: "## Asexual and Sexual\n\n**Algae reproduce both asexually (by binary fission, fragmentation, or spore formation) and sexually (by gamete fusion).**\n\nAsexual reproduction is common in single-celled algae. Binary fission produces two identical daughter cells. Budding produces a smaller cell that grows from the parent. Spore formation produces large numbers of small, motile spores that disperse and grow into new individuals.\n\nIn multicellular algae, fragmentation is common. A piece of the alga breaks off and grows into a new individual. Seaweeds often reproduce this way, a storm may tear off pieces of kelp, and those pieces can grow into new kelp if they settle in a suitable place.\n\nSexual reproduction involves the fusion of gametes (sex cells). The gametes can be morphologically similar (isogamy), different in size (anisogamy), or very different (oogamy, with a large non-motile egg and a small motile sperm). The zygote develops into a new individual.\n\nMany algae alternate between haploid and diploid generations, a pattern called alternation of generations. The haploid generation produces gametes; the diploid generation produces spores. This alternation is common in marine algae and in land plants (which inherited it from their algal ancestors).\n\nThe reproductive modes allow algae to exploit different strategies. Asexual reproduction produces large numbers quickly; sexual reproduction produces genetic variation for adaptation.\n\n> People often say 'only asexual', sexual reproduction is common in algae, especially in multicellular forms."
  },
  {
    course: "BI110",
    term: "T2",
    front: "Why are algae important in food webs?",
    back: "## The Base of the Web\n\n**Algae are the primary producers of aquatic food webs, they capture sunlight and convert it to organic matter that other organisms eat.**\n\nIn freshwater environments, green algae and cyanobacteria are the dominant producers. They grow in ponds, lakes, and streams, where they are eaten by zooplankton (small animals), which are eaten by small fish, which are eaten by larger fish and birds. Without the algae, the whole chain would collapse.\n\nIn marine environments, phytoplankton (mostly diatoms, dinoflagellates, and cyanobacteria) are the dominant producers. They are so abundant that they form visible blooms in some areas, and they are the base of the marine food web that supports fish, marine mammals, and seabirds.\n\nAlgae are also a direct food source for some organisms. Kelp is eaten by sea urchins, which are eaten by sea otters. Nori is eaten by humans in Japan. Spirulina (a cyanobacterium) is eaten as a dietary supplement.\n\nThe importance of algae is hard to overstate. They produce roughly half of the oxygen in the atmosphere. They fix carbon, removing CO₂ from the atmosphere. They support fisheries that feed billions of people. They are the foundation of the aquatic biosphere."
  },
  {
    course: "BI110",
    term: "T2",
    front: "What are the differences between the main algal phyla?",
    back: "## Pigments and Storage\n\n**The main algal phyla differ in pigmentation, storage product, cell wall composition, and habitat.**\n\nChlorophyta (green algae): Chlorophyll a and b, store starch, cellulose walls, mostly freshwater. Examples: Spirogyra, Chlamydomonas, Ulva.\n\nRhodophyta (red algae): Chlorophyll a and phycoerythrin (red pigment), store floridean starch, cellulose walls with sulfated polysaccharides, marine. Examples: Porphyra (nori), Gelidium.\n\nPhaeophyta (brown algae): Chlorophyll a and c plus fucoxanthin (brown pigment), store laminarin and mannitol, cellulose and algin walls, marine. Examples: kelp, Sargassum.\n\nChrysophyta (diatoms): Chlorophyll a and c plus fucoxanthin, store oils, silica walls (frustules), freshwater and marine. Examples: various diatom genera.\n\nPyrrophyta (dinoflagellates): Chlorophyll a and c plus peridinin, store starch and oils, cellulose plates, marine. Examples: Gonyaulax, Alexandrium.\n\nThe pigmentation determines the colour of the alga and the range of light it can absorb. Storage products determine how the alga manages its energy reserves. Cell wall composition determines its structure and durability. Habitat determines where it lives."
  },
  {
    course: "BI110",
    term: "T2",
    front: "How does algal pigmentation affect depth distribution?",
    back: "## Different Colours, Different Depths\n\n**Algal pigmentation determines which wavelengths of light are absorbed, which affects how deep the alga can grow.**\n\nWater filters light. Red light (long wavelength) is absorbed first, so it disappears in the first few metres. Blue-green light (short wavelength) penetrates deeper. The deeper you go, the more the light spectrum shifts toward blue-green.\n\nDifferent pigments absorb different wavelengths. Chlorophyll a and b absorb mainly red and blue light. Phycoerythrin (red algae) absorbs blue-green light. Fucoxanthin (brown algae) absorbs blue-green light. The diversity of pigments allows algae to exploit different light environments.\n\nGreen algae are usually found in shallow water, where red light is available. Red algae are found at greater depths, where their phycoerythrin can absorb the penetrating blue-green light. Brown algae are intermediate, found in the mid-tide zone.\n\nThis depth distribution is not absolute, many factors (nutrients, temperature, competition) also determine where algae grow. But pigmentation is one factor, and it explains why different coloured algae tend to be found at different depths.\n\nThe principle is the same as coloured filters in photography. Each pigment is a filter that captures only certain wavelengths. The alga that captures the most abundant wavelengths at its depth has an advantage."
  }
