    front: "How do protists contribute to carbon cycling?",
    back: "## The Biological Carbon Pump\n\n**Protists contribute to carbon cycling by fixing carbon dioxide (algae) and by transferring carbon to the deep ocean when they die and sink.**\n\nAlgae capture CO₂ during photosynthesis, converting it into organic matter. This is the entry point for carbon into the aquatic food web. The more algae grow, the more carbon they remove from the atmosphere.\n\nWhen algae die, some of their biomass sinks to the deep ocean. This process, the biological carbon pump, moves carbon from the surface to the deep ocean, where it can remain for centuries or longer. Without this pump, atmospheric CO₂ would be much higher.\n\nSome algae have hard shells (diatoms, coccolithophores) that sink rapidly after death. The shells are made of calcium carbonate (coccolithophores) or silica (diatoms), and they carry carbon with them. When the shells dissolve or are buried in sediment, the carbon is stored long-term.\n\nProtists also consume and respire carbon. Protozoans eat algae and other organic matter, releasing CO₂ back into the water. This is part of the carbon cycle, carbon moves between organisms, the water, and the atmosphere.\n\nThe balance between carbon fixation (by algae) and carbon release (by respiration) determines whether the ocean is a net sink or source of CO₂. Currently, the ocean is a net sink, it absorbs more CO₂ than it releases, and this absorption is driven partly by algal photosynthesis."
