    front: "Why are protists studied in medicine?",
    back: "## Pathogens and Models\n\n**Protists are studied in medicine both because some cause serious disease and because others serve as model organisms for understanding cell biology.**\n\nThe pathogenic protists, Plasmodium, Trypanosoma, Entamoeba, Giardia, and others, cause diseases that affect hundreds of millions of people each year. Understanding their biology is essential for developing treatments, vaccines, and diagnostic tests. Much of the research on these organisms is done in medical schools and research institutes.\n\nPlasmodium is a particularly important focus. Malaria is one of the deadliest infectious diseases, and drug resistance is a growing problem. Understanding the parasite's life cycle and metabolism is essential for developing new drugs and vaccines.\n\nThe non-pathogenic protists are also medically relevant. They serve as model organisms for understanding basic cellular processes, cell division, membrane trafficking, flagellar movement, chemotaxis. Many of these processes are conserved across all eukaryotes, including humans, so studying protists can illuminate human cell biology.\n\nSome protists are used in drug discovery. Marine protists produce novel chemicals that may have pharmaceutical applications. The anti-cancer compound from Lyngbya is one example.\n\nFinally, some protists are used as tools in biomedical research. Chlamydomonas is used to study cilia and flagella, which are relevant to human diseases like primary ciliary dyskinesia. Dictyostelium is used to study cell migration and chemotaxis, which are relevant to cancer metastasis."
